@@ -0,0 +1,125 @@
+# Native qualified GPU candidate budget filter v1
+
+Pinned base: `bdf434e5e9201cccf4e05fad04d814363653f692`. Plugin
+`nativequalifiedbudget` is an opt-in scheduler candidate filter, not hardware
+qualification, Source authorization, physical GPU selection, cap authority or a
+replacement scheduler. Existing scheduler configuration is unchanged.
+
+## Integration
+
+The plugin registers one GPUOrderFn. Returning an error removes that candidate
+(`pkg/scheduler/framework/session.go:213–225`, `session_plugins.go:496–506`).
+Ordinary Pods without the native pending marker always receive score 0. Native
+v2 requests fail closed while disabled, misconfigured or missing trusted inputs.
+KAI retains its existing GPU scoring, group creation, Statement allocation and
+resource-reservation/device-plugin physical selection.
+
+Known groups resolve only through one actual reservation Pod in the configured
+reservation namespace, matching Node and service account, GPU request/limit 1,
+original UID/RV and group label. Its `run.ai/reserve_for_gpu_index` annotation
+must resolve through the qualified inventory. Uniqueness is namespace-wide,
+matching the binder's lookup. Group IDs, array order and index 0 are not UUIDs.
+
+## Closed producer contract
+
+`pkg/scheduler/plugins/nativequalifiedbudget/testdata/inputs.schema.json` defines
+three closed JSON documents. The neighboring budget/request/claims fixtures and
+independently Python-computed canonical hashes define the interoperable wire
+shape. The reader additionally checks relational invariants; schema validation
+alone never grants authority. Duplicate keys, case aliases, unknown fields,
+trailing documents and payloads over 1 MiB are rejected.
+
+1. **Root budget:** a Root/operator compiler derives measured per-device caps,
+   budgets/counts and profile support from the typed qualified catalog. The
+   scheduler independently pins the immutable ConfigMap's complete reference
+   (namespace/name/UID/RV/data hash/key). It binds catalog/policy, Node UID,
+   boot/driver epoch, full advertised UUID/runtime-ID inventory, each device's
+   original qualification reference, and exact claims ConfigMap UID. Distinct
+   device qualification namespaces preserve independent authority ownership.
+2. **Gateway request:** only a Gateway/Source controller after actual Pod UID
+   registration may create this immutable document in a separately configured
+   Gateway-write namespace. It binds retained attempt/member/head/window intent
+   to actual Pod namespace/name/UID, profile/cap, unplaced hash, policy/catalog,
+   Source reference, head revision and authorization deadline. Its deterministic
+   name is `cps-native-pool-request-<PodUID>`. Node publishers cannot invent it.
+   There is no invalid cross-namespace Pod ownerReference. This plugin compares
+   request identity/hash/cap against the actual resource info retained by KAI;
+   later Gateway/Binder checks still authenticate the complete executable spec.
+3. **Node claims:** a trusted Node publisher supplies actual active/retiring
+   claims, exact projection/epoch, issued/expires times and canonical Pod/attempt
+   identities in the separately configured Node-write namespace. Claims remain
+   until actual native-zero proof: expiry never frees capacity. Missing, stale,
+   future, contradictory or incomplete inputs exclude that Node from new native
+   placement. Other healthy Nodes can remain eligible. Global attempt/Pod
+   duplicates must be exact; requests and retained claims must agree.
+
+ConfigMap `data_sha256` is SHA256 of compact, sorted-key ASCII JSON of the whole
+one-key data object, including the exact document string (and any newline).
+Reference digests hash compact sorted-key JSON of all six reference fields.
+Profile labels use `cps.compute/native-pool-<first16 sha256(JSON(profile))>`;
+values are the first 32 hex digits of the canonical catalog-reference digest.
+Budget/request CMs must be immutable; all input CMs have one data key, no binary
+payload, deletion timestamp or owner references. Claims can be mutable.
+
+Explicit arguments (all strings):
+
+| Argument | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `enabled` | `false` default; exactly `true` to opt in |
+| `budgetReference` | JSON full immutable reference, key `budget.json` |
+| `requestNamespace` | independently trusted Gateway request namespace |
+| `claimsNamespace` | separate trusted Node claims namespace |
+| `reservationNamespace` | actual KAI reservation namespace |
+| `reservationServiceAccount` | actual reservation Pod service account |
+| `maxSnapshotAgeSeconds` | required integer 1..300 when enabled |
+
+The existing raw shared informers supply metadata and data. SessionOpen parses
+and detaches the closed snapshot; scoring does no network I/O or JSON parsing.
+Watch-maintained atomic generation invalidates a session when relevant inputs
+change. Scoring also checks local original UID/RV for budget/request/claims,
+actual Pod, Node and reservations and rechecks freshness/deadline. Graph Node
+and raw Node versions must agree; an older graph cannot borrow a newer raw
+Node's RV. No new informers, RBAC or publisher activation is installed here.
+
+These are local watch observations, not a distributed SQL-to-Kubernetes
+transaction or a final authorization permit. Watch delivery latency, a later
+head transition or a device assignment change must be rejected by the future
+fresh admission/Binding protocol. Scheduler placement alone cannot start GPU
+execution.
+
+## Conservative physical accounting
+
+Native Node eligibility requires every GPU advertised to KAI to be qualified for
+the requested profile/current epoch, ready/uncordoned, no MIG or DRA, and exact
+advertised GPU count/memory. Node inventory mismatch disables new native
+placement on that Node; non-native scheduling is unchanged.
+
+Accounting starts from active plus retiring claims and deduplicates exact claim
+and actual Pod identities. Known-group provisional allocations consume that
+physical device's measured MiB and workspace count. Foreign ordinary sharing
+on a group excludes native sharing there. CPU Pod group labels do not charge a
+GPU. Allocate/Deallocate invalidate a local view recomputed from actual NodeInfo
+assignments; real Statement rollback removes only provisional charges.
+
+WholeGpuIndicator creates an opaque group; the device plugin may choose any
+unreserved physical GPU. Every possible unreserved device must fit each unknown
+group's accumulated MiB/count and all profiles. Distinct unknown groups occupy
+distinct slots, without summing unrelated groups onto every GPU. This permits
+two safe new groups on two slots and preserves known-safe B beside a separate
+unresolved A. It intentionally sacrifices degraded-card availability rather
+than pretending the scheduler chooses an unobservable physical UUID.
+
+## Verification and remaining activation gates
+
+Tests exercise actual FittingGPUs → KAI choice (A full, B safe), real Statement
+Allocate/Rollback, independent MiB/count limits, duplicate/retiring custody,
+multiple unresolved groups, stale/foreign inputs, exact reservation identity,
+long-session expiry, watch invalidation, ordinary/default-off behavior and no
+API calls in scoring. Static fixtures are synthetic CPU contracts, not measured
+hardware evidence.
+
+Trusted producers, current Source/head/ACK/epoch admission, reservation native-
+zero retirement guard, startup/cap publication, all-eight-device qualification
+and rollout are separate mandatory work. The existing advertised-inventory
+patch and separate binder UID/RV deletion fix must be integrated and qualified
+independently; neither is silently incorporated in this pinned-base change.
